    "reservation": {
      "max_pct": 100,
      "note": "The player opens auras; the only system rule is that total reserved mana may not reach 100% of the pool — some mana must stay usable for skills. There is no other cap."
    },
    "formula": {
      "mana_level_step_pct": 5,
      "mana_pool_exponent": 0.5,
      "mana_reference_level": 1,
      "cast_reference": {
        "cdr_pct": 50,
        "ladder_pct": 30
      },
      "note": "press = (base_flat + (eff/100) x power) x (1 + (skill_level - 1) x level_step_pct/100) - the PoE shape: `base_flat` is the skill own damage at skill level 1 (its identity, the same for every caster), `eff` is the share of the added power line the press receives, so gear scales a row by its effectiveness instead of by a percentage of the finished hit. power = the row basis - phys = the caster finished physical hit, magic = their magic + Element x Alignment/100 (B5). `archetype_bands` is the calibration the pairs were derived from: a row damage per second is set by its archetype and the pinned maximum is the roster own best cooldown row per basis, so the DPS line the curve prices does not move while a press reads as a heavy or a light hit. Read by engine/skills.ts `perPress` - the --calc workshop, the attack-table press column and the client casts all call it, so a press is never computed twice. A mana cost is written in one of two forms and the unit is part of it: `N%` charges N% of the usable pool, `N flat` charges N absolute units. A flat cost is the price at skill level 1 and grows on `mana_level_step_pct` per skill level, steeper than the damage ramp so a maxed rotation costs more, and on `(pool / reference pool)^mana_pool_exponent`, so Int and Max Mana gear still set the price. The reference pool is derived at `mana_reference_level`, never typed here.",
      "archetype_bands": {
        "note": "The calibration, not a formula: a row damage per second is set by what it is — a slow single-target nuke sits at the top of its power line, an area row pays a penalty for the targets it reaches, a fast filler sits below — and its per-press is then that rate inverted, so a press reads as a heavy or a light hit instead of every row pressing the same. `base_share` is how much of the press is the row own flat (a nuke) versus its effectiveness on the caster power line (a filler). The pinned maximum is the roster own best cooldown row per basis, so the DPS line the curve prices does not move. The cooldown-less beat row keeps its own press.",
        "bands": {
          "nuke": 1,
          "mid": 0.97,
          "filler": 0.94,
          "aoe_penalty": -0.12
        },
        "base_share": {
          "nuke": 0.6,
          "mid": 0.45,
          "filler": 0.3
        }
      },
      "skill_levels": {
        "note": "One row per skill level: the multiplier a press and an effect carry at that level. `damage_pct` is the ramp the roster always had; `effect_pct` is the lever that used to be absent (a value in a row is its level-1 number, and it climbs with this column). `special_level_effects` lists the effect stats that opt out of the ramp: Energy Absorb interpolates its base toward its cap across the level range, and Ghost Dance counts a charge every N levels, so both already spend skill level and must not be scaled twice. Row 1 is the FLOOR a fresh skill starts on, not the finished value: a skill dropped at level 1 presses a quarter of what it will, and grows into its press as it levels.",
